---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RotateVideo from '@/components/tools/media/RotateVideo.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="rotate-video">
  <RotateVideo client:load mode="rotate" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Why phone video ends up sideways</h2>
    <p>
      A phone camera sensor is fixed in one orientation and always records landscape frames. When you hold
      the phone upright, the camera app does not turn the picture. It records the same landscape frames and
      writes a <strong>rotation flag</strong> into the file that says "show this turned 90 degrees". Your
      photo app reads the flag and shows the video the right way up. Software that ignores the flag shows it
      sideways.
    </p>
    <p>
      Old media players, some upload forms, some web video pipelines and some TVs ignore the flag. The
      footage is not damaged; only the instruction is lost.
    </p>

    <h2>Two ways to rotate a video</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>Rewrite the rotation flag</th>
            <th>Write it into the pixels</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>What changes</td>
            <td>One metadata field</td>
            <td>Each frame is decoded, turned and re-encoded</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Speed on a 2 GB file</td>
            <td>Seconds</td>
            <td>About real time</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Quality</td>
            <td>Unchanged, bit-for-bit identical</td>
            <td>One generation of H.264 loss</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Works in</td>
            <td>Software that reads the flag</td>
            <td>All software</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Container support</td>
            <td>MP4, MOV, 3GP only</td>
            <td>Any input; the output is MP4</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Try the flag first, because it is fast and lossless. If the problem app still shows the video sideways,
      come back and write the rotation into the pixels. Then there is no flag left to ignore.
    </p>

    <h2>Which way should I turn it?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>90° clockwise</strong>: the top of the picture is on the left. This is the usual fix for a phone held with the volume buttons up.</li>
      <li><strong>90° counter-clockwise</strong>: the top of the picture is on the right.</li>
      <li><strong>180°</strong>: the video is upside down. Usually the phone was held the other way up, or the clip came from a dashcam or action camera mounted upside down.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The live preview above the button applies the rotation with CSS, so you can check the direction
      before you start.
    </p>

    <h2>A quarter turn swaps the resolution</h2>
    <p>
      A 90-degree rotation turns 1920×1080 into 1080×1920: the same pixels in a different shape. The tool
      shows the exact output dimensions before you start. A 180° turn keeps the dimensions.
    </p>

    <h2>Rotating is not mirroring</h2>
    <p>
      If text in the frame reads backwards, or a selfie recording is the wrong way round, the video is
      mirrored. Fix it on the <a href="/flip-video">flip video</a> page. Rotation only turns the frame; it
      never swaps left and right.
    </p>

    <h2>Runs in this tab</h2>
    <p>
      The rotation runs in this tab through FFmpeg compiled to WebAssembly, so your personal recordings stay
      on your device. With the flag-only method, a multi-gigabyte video is fixed in seconds.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
